---
/* THE MASTHEAD (CONTEXT.md), and it is now the site's furniture rather than the
   index's first screen. See docs/adr/0011.

   IT WAS HAND-COPIED INTO TWO FILES. index.astro and About.astro carried
   near-identical markup and ~140 lines of restated CSS, and About.astro:143 said
   why in as many words: a shared component was refused because the About band
   reused the Plates' own `.ordinal` / `.title` / `.spec` rules, and lifting the
   Masthead out of index.astro would either drag those with it or fork them —
   "worth revisiting if a third page ever opens this way". Six Project pages and
   a 404 are that third page, and Meta.astro is what resolves the blocker: the
   band is its own component now, so both objects reference one set of rules.

   ONE THING VARIES PER PAGE and it is the eyebrow. Everything else is identical
   on every route by construction rather than by discipline.

   IT IS NOT A PLATE, deliberately. The Plate contract is what makes six Projects
   differ in subject rather than in status, and a person entered into it would be
   exactly the status difference the contract removes. So this borrows the
   Plate's left datum edge and its metadata width, and nothing else: no ordinal
   on the header itself, no art-directed crop, and it sits outside the <ol>.

   WHAT MAKES IT BELONG ANYWAY is the specification line below the name. The
   datum is the specification line (docs/design-direction.md), so an element that
   carries one is measured by the same reference as everything under it, without
   being the same kind of object.

   WHERE A <nav> GOES: inside this <header>, after `.masthead-text`, as a sibling
   of it in the grid. It is not here yet because the site has no second-level
   routes to list and inventing one would be a mark the design does not use. What
   this file has already settled for it is the part that is expensive to change
   later — the element is a <header> and a direct child of <body>, so it is a
   `banner` landmark and a <nav> inside it is site navigation. A <nav> inside
   <main>, which is where this markup used to live, is page content instead. */
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import Meta from './Meta.astro';
import portrait from '../assets/portrait.jpg';
import { ABOUT_PATH, PRACTICE_LEDE, PRACTICE_SPEC } from '../site';
import {
  portraitSizes,
  PORTRAIT_WIDTH,
  PORTRAIT_WIDTHS,
  FRAME_FORMATS,
  FRAME_FALLBACK,
} from '../layout';

interface Props {
  /** What the screen below this one is: `Selected work`, `About`, `Project`,
      `Not found`. Required, with no default — a new page decides what it is the
      same way it decides `noindex`, and a default here would let a route ship
      silently mislabelled. Sentence case; `.t-label` uppercases it. */
  eyebrow: string;
}

const { eyebrow } = Astro.props;

/* THE BAND IS DERIVED, NOT PASSED, and that is the difference between a rule and
   a convention. On /about/ime/ the About block would be a control leading to the
   document it is already in, so it is suppressed there — and a prop would make
   that every future page's job to remember. `ABOUT_PATH` carries the note about
   why the trailing slash is load-bearing. */
const about = Astro.url.pathname !== ABOUT_PATH;
---

{/* REGISTRATION, TIER A, AND NO --d. ADR-0010: an animation-delay on the LCP
    element is added to LCP one for one, and on a phone this header IS roughly
    the first screen — so whichever element inside it is largest, the delay would
    land on the LCP element. Measured, that element is the portrait on most
    routes and the positioning paragraph on /about/ime/; either way it is in
    here. So this starts on the first frame on every route.

    ONE data-anim ON THE WHOLE HEADER, not one per line. It is one screen
    arriving, not five elements; the staggered version read as the page
    assembling itself. */}
<header class="masthead" data-anim="now">
  <div class="masthead-text">
    <span class="t-label eyebrow">{eyebrow}</span>

    {/* THE SITE'S ONE h1, ON EVERY PAGE. tokens.css's rule is that h1 is the
        site's name and h2 is a page's own title, and this is what makes that
        rule true everywhere rather than only on `/`: a Project's heading and
        /404's are h2 now, both already at --t-h2, so nothing moved except the
        tag. */}
    <h1>Coulter Heiberger</h1>

    {/* ADR-0005: the display line is `Technical Artist`, unqualified. Not
        "Technical Artist / Architectural Visualization", not a parenthetical.
        Qualifiers go on the specification line below, where they read as scope
        rather than as a job title that could not decide what it was. */}
    <p class="t-label role">Technical Artist</p>

    <p class="positioning">{PRACTICE_LEDE}</p>

    <p class="t-spec spec">{PRACTICE_SPEC}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="masthead-aside">
    {/* <Picture>, not the Plate's hand-assembled <picture>. That component
        exists to switch COMPOSITION across the mode boundary, and a square
        portrait has one composition — so the component that only switches format
        is the correct one, which is the same call a Project's gallery makes for
        a Frame.

        AVIF WITH A WEBP FALLBACK, from the same two constants the gallery uses.
        This element sits in the first viewport, so it is fetched immediately
        whatever `loading` says, and the gallery's own measurement was a 53% cut
        moving WebP q80 to AVIF q50. Keeping the formats in layout.ts is also
        what stops CI's processed-image cache going stale on them.

        EAGER, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A DEFAULT. This element was
        `loading="lazy"` for as long as the Masthead was one page's header, on the
        argument that a 320px square is never the LCP element. Putting the
        Masthead on every route falsified that: measured under the budget's own
        configuration, the portrait became the LCP element on ALL NINE pages, and
        an LCP element that is lazy is the one thing ADR-0010 says never to ship.
        Median LCP, mobile, devtools throttling, median of 3:

                          lazy     eager
          /               2002     1761
          /about/ime/     1634     1511
          /projects/...   1974     1954
          /404            1585     1575

        `loading="lazy"` was never holding it back in the first viewport anyway —
        Chrome's lazy threshold widens as the connection slows — it was only
        denying the preload scanner the chance to start it early.

        STILL NOT `priority`, and that is the line that did not move. `priority`
        would add `fetchpriority="high"`, and on a Project page that would put a
        face in direct competition with the hero, which is the LCP element on a
        landscape window and is the work. Eager buys back most of the regression
        without claiming the slot; the remaining ~1.6s is the honest cost of
        putting a face on every route, and every page clears the 2500ms gate.

        /404 IS THE ONE THAT BARELY MOVED, and the reason is that it has almost
        no other content: the portrait genuinely is the largest thing on it. 686ms
        to 1575ms is what a 404 with a face costs. See ADR-0011.

        IT IS THE ONE NEW BYTE ON EVERY PAGE'S LCP PATH. If a route regresses
        under `npm run budget`, look here first. */}
    <Picture
      src={portrait}
      alt="Coulter Heiberger, smiling, in glasses and a light blue collared shirt against a warm stucco wall."
      width={PORTRAIT_WIDTH}
      height={PORTRAIT_WIDTH}
      widths={PORTRAIT_WIDTHS}
      sizes={portraitSizes}
      formats={FRAME_FORMATS}
      fallbackFormat={FRAME_FALLBACK}
      loading="eager"
      decoding="async"
    />

    {/* THE PORTRAIT IS THE LINK, and this band is how it says so.

        IT WAS AN ORDINARY BORDERED LINK SET UNDER THE FACE, on the argument that
        "this element has two things in it a visitor might want and only one of
        them is a link". Owner review overturned the premise: there are not two
        things here, there is one offer — go read about him — and splitting it
        across a face and a word underneath made the face inert and made the word
        the only affordance on `/` drawn with a border rather than with the
        datum.

        SO IT IS A PLATE'S BAND, literally: the same component the six Projects
        render, with the same four parts. `00` because the practice precedes the
        work and the Plates start at 01; `Various` and `1999` because that is
        what the About "project" is made of and when it started. */}
    {about && (
      <Meta ordinal={0} title="About" href={ABOUT_PATH} spec="Various" year={1999} />
    )}
  </div>
</header>

<style>
  /* THE SHELL'S MEASURE IS IN base.css, shared with <main> and the footer — see
     the rule on `body > header` there. Only the padding is this element's, and
     it is open at the bottom because `.masthead` carries --rhythm as a margin
     instead. Top of viewport -> masthead is --rhythm, masthead -> content is
     --rhythm, which is what `/` and /about/ime/ measured before this component
     existed. */
  /* ---- the arrangement ----------------------------------------------------
     Two columns, text then face, and the right-hand one is --plate-meta.

     IT USED TO BE "the Plate's grid with the columns swapped", and the grid it
     was swapping is gone: a Plate is one full-width band with no metadata column
     beside it. What survives is the width and the shared left datum edge.
     --plate-meta has exactly this one consumer, which is why the token's comment
     says so rather than describing a column that no longer exists. */
  .masthead {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s4);
    padding: var(--rhythm) var(--gap) 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--rhythm);
  }

  /* Same condition as the Plates, the crops, and tokens.css's --gap switch: a
     SHAPE, not a width. See WIDE_MODE in src/layout.ts.

     The portrait column is placed explicitly rather than reordered: DOM order is
     the reading order, and `order` would make the visual arrangement contradict
     it rather than place it. Here the text is first in both, so this is only a
     placement. */
  @media (min-aspect-ratio: 1 / 1) {
    .masthead {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) var(--plate-meta);
      gap: var(--gap);

      /* `stretch`, not `start`. This is what gives the portrait column the text
         block's height for the rule under `.masthead-aside` to fill. It is the
         grid default, and it is written out because it is load-bearing here:
         restoring `start` would silently collapse the face back to a square and
         the change would look like nothing more than a shorter image. */
      align-items: stretch;
    }
  }

  .eyebrow {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: var(--s3);
  }

  /* The only --t-h1 on the site. Tracking comes from base.css at -0.02em; this
     rule used to restate it as -0.022em, a difference of two thousandths of an
     em that no one could see and that made the site's largest heading the one
     place the shared value did not apply. */
  h1 {
    font-size: var(--t-h1);
    line-height: 0.96;
  }

  /* RESERVE THE SECOND LINE ON PHONES, AND IT IS A LAYOUT-SHIFT FIX RATHER THAN
     A SPACING CHOICE.

     `font-display: swap` on the display face means the name paints in a fallback
     and then swaps, and Montserrat is much wider: measured in a browser at the
     clamped 38.4px this band uses, "Coulter Heiberger" is 348px in Montserrat
     against 303px in the fallback. The column is `vw - 55`, so between 360px and
     402px THE FALLBACK FITS ON ONE LINE AND MONTSERRAT DOES NOT. When the
     download landed the h1 went 36.86px -> 73.72px, taking the portrait and
     everything below down 37px with it — a 0.0128 shift arriving in the middle
     of Registration's entrance, which is what made the top of the page look like
     it was snapping into place.

     That band is 375, 390 and 393: iPhone, iPhone Pro Max and Pixel. It is
     invisible at every desktop width, so this is a bug you cannot see where you
     develop. Below 360 both faces wrap and this is a no-op; at 403 and up
     neither wraps and the rule is off, which is why the query stops there rather
     than at a round breakpoint.

     WHY NOT FIX IT IN THE FONT LAYER. Two ways were tried and both were rejected
     on evidence. A metric-matched fallback (size-adjust + ascent-override on a
     local() face) is the textbook answer and needs one ratio per platform
     fallback — but a headless container has neither Arial nor Helvetica Neue and
     silently measures the default sans while returning entirely plausible
     numbers, so the ratios cannot be verified anywhere in this project's
     toolchain, and a wrong ratio reintroduces the shift while looking like a
     fix. `font-display: optional` was measured here and did not hold: Chrome
     applied the face at t=1551ms with the request delayed 1.5s. Reserving the
     box needs no font metrics and no per-platform anything.

     THE 403px IS DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN, and it is derived from a string in a
     column of a given width. The column is now this header's padding rather than
     <main>'s, and the two are the same --gap, so the number carries — but change
     the name, the gutters, --t-h1, or the Montserrat subset and re-measure it:
     nothing in CI can. The failure is silent and only on a phone. */
  @media (max-width: 403px) {
    h1 {
      min-height: calc(2 * 0.96 * var(--t-h1));
    }
  }

  /* THE ROLE IS A LABEL, and it says so. It was Montserrat at its own size,
     which made it a fourth heading size on a page that already had two — and the
     argument for it ("a title, not reading, in the same voice as the name") is
     equally an argument for the mono datum voice, which is where the Social Card
     has always set this exact string.

     So the eyebrow and the role bracket the name in the same style: what the
     page is above it, who the site is below it, and one very large name between.
     Everything else here is furniture in the same 11-12px mono, which is the
     whole point of a datum. */
  .role {
    margin: var(--s3) 0 0;
  }

  /* The positioning paragraph, and the reason the serif is above the fold at
     all. No font-size: it is body prose and it is set like body prose, which is
     the correction tokens.css records against the 15px summary size `/` used to
     mint for itself.

     --ink, WHICH IS THE INHERITED COLOUR AND THEREFORE NOT DECLARED. This read
     --muted for a long time, on the argument that a lede is secondary to the
     name above it. Overturned on owner review: it is the only prose on the first
     screen and it is the sentence a visitor reads to find out what this is, so
     it is primary text and takes the primary colour.

     --body-col rather than the column's full width. The wide column runs to
     ~1230px at the page cap, and 17px Georgia across that is far past any
     reading measure. */
  .positioning {
    margin: var(--s3) 0 0;
    max-width: var(--body-col);
  }

  /* NO COLOUR OVERRIDE. This was --ink for one draft, to give the practice's own
     line more weight than the six below it. That is exactly the move base.css
     refuses by name: a type style that reads one way in one place and another
     way next to it is not a style, and it is how the footer address became the
     site's tenth treatment. The specification line is one style, and the
     Masthead's is a specification line. Margin only.

     THE TWO BANDS DO OVERRIDE IT, and this rule is still right. The difference
     is not that they were allowed a preference and this line was not: over an
     image the text colour SETS THE SCRIM'S ALPHA FLOOR, so it stops being a
     colour choice and becomes a legibility one — --muted would force 0.94 and
     leave no fade. The derivation is on --scrim in Meta.astro. On the Ground
     there is no such forcing function, which is why this line has nothing to
     trade and keeps the shared colour.

     IT IS A BARE `.spec` NOW, not `.masthead .spec`. The (0,2,0) was there to
     beat the Plates' bare `.spec` inside this header, which was a real collision
     while both lived in index.astro: it left the About band at the wide 20px
     while the six Plates sat at 8px. Astro scopes every compound in a selector,
     so the band's rules and this one no longer see each other and the weight has
     nothing left to win against. */
  .spec {
    margin: var(--s3) 0 0;
  }

  /* ---- the portrait column ------------------------------------------------
     THE CAP IS MIRRORED IN `portraitSizes` (src/layout.ts) so the width the CSS
     draws and the width the browser fetches cannot drift.

     200px BELOW THE MODE SWITCH AND NOT THE FULL CONTENT WIDTH. A face at 100vw
     is the entire first screen on a phone, it would take the LCP element off the
     work and onto the author, and it would push the content a screen and a half
     down. The cap is the whole reason this reads as a masthead rather than as a
     profile page.

     WIDTH ON THE CONTAINER RATHER THAN A CAP ON THE IMAGE, because the band has
     to be the width of the face and not the width of the column.

     THE PORTRAIT AND ITS BAND SHARE ONE GRID CELL. Same stacking shape as a
     Plate, and adopted for the same two reasons: the band can be revealed
     without moving anything, and this element stays the positioned ancestor so
     `.title a::after` resolves against the whole face instead of against the
     band. Meta.astro's note records what getting that wrong costs, because it
     had already shipped once.

     `isolation: isolate` confines the band's z-index to this column. Without it
     that `z-index: 1` competes in the root stacking context, because `position:
     relative` with `z-index: auto` does not open one. */
  .masthead-aside {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    display: grid;
    width: 200px;
  }

  /* <picture> is inline by default, which puts a descender's worth of gap under
     it. `:global()` because the element comes from a component. */
  .masthead-aside :global(picture) {
    display: block;
    grid-area: 1 / 1;
  }

  .masthead-aside :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* THE FACE MATCHES THE HEIGHT OF THE PROSE BESIDE IT, asked for directly at
     owner review: the two columns read as one object rather than as a square
     parked next to a paragraph.

     THE PORTRAIT IS TAKEN OUT OF FLOW TO DO IT, and that is the whole mechanism
     rather than a detail. `align-items: stretch` alone does nothing: while the
     <picture> is an in-flow grid item it is the TALLEST item in the row, so it
     sizes the row and then "fills" a height it set itself. The first attempt
     looked like it worked only because a 320px square and the prose beside it
     happened to land within 17px of each other. Absolute breaks the circle — out
     of flow it contributes nothing, the row is the TEXT's height, and the face
     fills that. Ask for the face to drive the height again and this is the
     declaration to remove, not `align-items`.

     `height: 100%` has to beat base.css's `img { height: auto }`, and dropping
     `aspect-ratio` is what lets it: with both set the ratio wins and the stretch
     is silently ignored. `object-fit: cover` absorbs the difference.

     BOTH CONDITIONS, not just the shape. The mode switch is an aspect ratio, so
     an 844x390 landscape phone reaches the wide branch; stretching a face to a
     text column there makes it the largest thing on screen. Below 1200px the
     portrait keeps its square and the column simply has air in it — which is
     also the boundary Meta.astro gates the band on, for the same reason. */
  @media (min-aspect-ratio: 1 / 1) and (min-width: 1200px) {
    .masthead-aside {
      width: auto;
    }

    .masthead-aside :global(picture) {
      position: absolute;
      inset: 0;
    }

    .masthead-aside :global(img) {
      height: 100%;
      aspect-ratio: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
